                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Input is solid colored grid with center block a different color. Center block: center cell (or center area) has different color. Output: use center color, starting at each corner of center block, make diagonal lines to each corner (of the grid).\n\nFor a 3x3 grid, center cell is at (1,1). Corners of center block = itself. Diagonals from center to grid corners: (0,0),(1,1),(2,2) and (0,2),(1,1),(2,0). So output colors at those diagonal positions with center color, rest unchanged.\n\nExample: input 3x3 all 6 except center 0. Output: 0 at (0,0),(0,2),(1,1),(2,0),(2,2).\n\nNow I need to create demonstration pairs. I should create various sizes. Note center block is at halve(shape) \u2014 the center pixel index (h//2, w//2). For odd dimensions, center is unique. Let's use odd dims like 3,5,7.\n\nLet me create a 5x5 example. Center at (2,2). Input solid color say 3 with center 7. Output: diagonals from center to corners. Lines: from (2,2) shoot UP_RIGHT direction (-1,1): goes to (0,4)... wait shoot starts at center and goes 42 steps in direction. UP_RIGHT=(-1,1). So line from (2,2) to (2-42, 2+42) = negative, but connect only fills within? Actually fill checks bounds 0<=i<h, 0<=j<w. connect from (2,2) to (-40,44): direction... let's compute. ai=2,aj=2; bi=-40,bj=44. si=min=-40,ei=max+1=3; sj=min=2,ej=45. bi-ai=-42, bj-aj=42. aj-bj? bi-ai == aj-bj => -42 == 2-44=-42 yes. So diagonal from (i,j) zip(range(-40,3), range(44,1,-1))... Actually for i in si..ei-1: -40..2, j from ej-1 down: 44,43,... So pairs: (-40,44),...,(2,2). In-bounds cells: those with 0<=i<5 and 0<=j<5. At i=0, j=? Let's see i+j=4 => (0,4),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(4,0). That's the anti-diagonal. Wait UP_RIGHT gives anti-diagonal (top-right to bottom-left) passing through center. DOWN_LEFT=(1,-1) gives same line. So combining gives that anti-diagonal.\n\nUNITY=(1,1) and NEG_UNITY=(-1,-1) give main diagonal (0,0),(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4).\n\nSo output: main diagonal and anti-diagonal from center to corners, colored with center color. That's an X shape.\n\nSo the rule: draw X through the center, using center color.\n\nLet me verify with 3x3: main diagonal (0,0),(1,1),(2,2); anti (0,2),(1,1),(2,0). Matches.\n\nSo for a 5x5: cells (0,0),(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4) and (0,4),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(4,0).\n\nNow the \"center block\" \u2014 for even dimensions, halve gives (h//2, w//2). Hmm but \"center block\" concept. The natural language says center block different color. Might be multiple center cells for even sizes? But verifier only picks one index. Let's stick to odd sizes for safety, or even too \u2014 verifier handles it, but then it wouldn't look like a symmetric X. Better use odd.\n\nActually wait \u2014 for even size like 4x4, center at (2,2). Diagonals from (2,2): main (0,0),(1,1),(2,2),(3,3); anti (0,4) out... (2,2) to (4,-2)? direction DOWN_LEFT. Let's just use odd sizes.\n\nLet me create a few pairs: 3x3 (different colors), 5x5, 7x7. Also maybe vary the \"center block\" being larger? But verifier only checks center pixel. Hmm.\n\nActually maybe the original ARC task ea786f4a had a center block that could be larger. But this verifier uses single index halve(shape). So just single center.\n\nLet me make pairs with different colors and sizes. I'll do 5x5, 7x7, and maybe 9x9.\n\nLet me be careful: input must be solid grid with center different. Provide input grid and environment computes output.\n\nLet's do:\n1) 5x5 all 2, center 4.\n2) 7x7 all 1, center 8.\n3) 3x3 all 5, center 9.\n4) 9x9 all 3, center 6.\n\nEnough. Let me submit.\n\nActually I should provide a variety to help infer. Let's do 4-5 examples.\n\nLet me ensure rectangular and valid.\n\nSubmit.",
